**Theorem 3.1 (the labelled stopped carrier).** For every base (QC.1),
calibrated preparation (QC.4), prescribed original control in Definition 1.2
and interval $[s,t]$, (QC.9)–(QC.11) form a complete countably additive
instrument. Equations (QC.6) give an exact correspondence with the original
five-live-mode instrument plus its absorbing state on
$V\oplus\mathbb Cf$. This correspondence holds for each success operation
and each of the five failure CP measures separately, with every actual joint
archive/reference/metadata register in Definition 1.3 retained.

It preserves legality, complete finite event traces, their joint terminal
states, clocks, selectors and updates under every finite policy in that
contract. Thus the live quantum algebra can be represented by $M_3$ with
a classical absorbing sector, or by its four-dimensional Hilbert embedding.
The result changes the specimen representation and supplies none of the
conditional services or missing acquisitions in Definition 1.3.

Proof. Every actual live transported vector belongs to $V$: its amplitudes
in modes $0,4$ have the common varying modulus $\sqrt{k}$ and common
increment $H_+$, with the fixed phases already in $U_+$; modes $1,3$ have
the corresponding common increment $H_-$ and modulus $\sqrt{c-k}$.
This argument also applies at open nonloop prefixes, where a real relation
phase need not be an integer multiple of $2\pi$. Thus $V$ is the same
fixed support at every live cut, not a subspace chosen using the hidden phase.

The maps in (QC.6) are CPTP. Compression to a subspace is CP, and the
complementary trace-and-prepare term is CP. Their trace contributions sum
to $\operatorname{tr}\rho$. The decoder is isometric and trace preserving.
For every operator $X$ supported on $P$,

$$
\mathcal D\mathcal E(X)=X,
\qquad \mathcal E\mathcal D=\operatorname{id}_{M_4}.
\tag{QC.13}
$$

These operator identities imply exact joint-state recovery after tensoring
with $\operatorname{id}_R$, including off-diagonal correlations with $R$.
They do not assume that the specimen and its archive are independent.

Strict positivity on this one compact path gives a positive minimum for
$k,c-k,Z$, so all logarithmic derivatives and all $\gamma$ in (QC.8) are
integrable. If $k'\geq0$, then $\lambda=k'/k$; if $k'\leq0$, then
$\lambda=-k'/(c-k)$. Both cases show $\gamma_+,\gamma_-,\gamma_2\geq0$.
For $\alpha\in\{+,-,2\}$ the absolutely continuous function
$q_\alpha(\tau)=|a_\alpha(\tau,s)|^2$ satisfies

$$
q_\alpha(s)=1,
\qquad q'_\alpha=-\gamma_\alpha q_\alpha\quad\hbox{a.e.}
\tag{QC.14}
$$

Consequently each diagonal entry of $\widehat A^\dagger\widehat A$ is
at most one. Integrating (QC.14) and summing both half-strength labels of
each paired mode gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=0}^4\int_s^t L_i(\tau;s)^\dagger L_i(\tau;s)\,d\tau
 &=\operatorname{diag}(1-|a_+|^2,1-|a_-|^2,1-|a_2|^2,0),\\
\widehat A_{t,s}^\dagger\widehat A_{t,s}
 +\sum_{i=0}^4\int_s^t L_i^\dagger L_i\,d\tau&=I_4.
\end{aligned}
\tag{QC.15}
$$

The integrable positive CP densities give countable additivity; (QC.15)
proves completeness without normalizing any separate failure branch.
At zero duration the integral is zero and $\widehat A=I_4$.

For exact operation matching, let the original jump density be the supplied

$$
J_i(\tau;s)=\sqrt{\gamma_i(\tau)}\,|f\rangle\langle i|K_{\tau,s},
\tag{QC.16}
$$

extended by zero on $f$. Here $\gamma_0=\gamma_4=\gamma_+$ and
$\gamma_1=\gamma_3=\gamma_-$. Directly on the four basis columns of $W$,

$$
A_{t,s}W=W\widehat A_{t,s},
\qquad J_i(\tau;s)W=WL_i(\tau;s)
\quad(i=0,\ldots,4)\quad\hbox{a.e.}
\tag{QC.17}
$$

For example, $\langle0|U_+\rangle=e^{-i\theta_0}/\sqrt2$ gives exactly
the first density in (QC.10), including its fixed phase. The other paired
columns have the same factor; the middle column has factor one. All other
columns of that jump are zero. The finite-interval operators satisfy the
same identity with the supplier's
$B_{t,s,i}=\sqrt{1-|K_{t,s,ii}|^2}|f\rangle\langle i|$
and (QC.12). Integrating (QC.17) proves, for every Borel $B$, every $i$
and every supported input $X$,

$$
\mathcal I_i(B)(X)
   =\mathcal D\widehat{\mathcal I}_i(B)\mathcal E(X).
\tag{QC.18}
$$

The same formula holds for the no-new-failure operation. This is equality
of CP operations on the entire supported matrix algebra, not only equality
of marginal outcome probabilities on the endpoint family. It remains true
with $\operatorname{id}_R$ on an arbitrary actual joint reference; the
finite specimen matrix expansion proves this also for normal trace-class
joint states. Classical state-valued measures are transported eventwise.

The coefficients obey
$a_\alpha(u,t)a_\alpha(t,s)=a_\alpha(u,s)$ by splitting the charge and
phase integrals. For a failure time $\tau>t$ the jump after survival to $t$
therefore equals the jump for the whole interval. A failure at $\tau\leq t$
has successor $f$, which is subsequently fixed. Splitting each labelled
failure integral at $t$ proves chronological consistency of the complete
instrument, including the retained first time and mode. When separate
controls are joined, `concatenate_original` supplies exactly the original
prefix, translated tail and phase integrals used in these identities.

Finally, at a policy cut the actual event and every retained field have the
same joint state under (QC.13) and (QC.18). An operation on the retained
registers commutes with $W$ on the specimen. The same selection rule and
external input therefore choose the same supplied next programme, or the
same permission rejection. Its clock and resource fields are the same
actual fields, not reconstructed values. Induction over a finite policy
proves joint trace and successor correspondence. Failed branches stay
absorbing. Decoding gives every terminal quantum read of the original
specimen with its actual reference. This proves the theorem. $\square$

